	abstract = {A sentiment analysis has received a lot of attention from researchers working in the fields of natural language processing and text mining. However, there is a lack of annotated data sets that can be used to train a model for all domains, which is hampering the accuracy of sentiment analysis. Many research studies have attempted to tackle this issue and to improve cross-domain sentiment classification. In this paper, we present the results of a comprehensive systematic literature review of the methods and techniques employed in a cross-domain sentiment analysis. We focus on studies published during the period of 2010-2016. From our analysis of those works, it is clear that there is no perfect solution. Hence, one of the aims of this review is to create a resource in the form of an overview of the techniques, methods, and approaches that have been used to attempt to solve the problem of cross-domain sentiment analysis in order to assist researchers in developing new and more accurate techniques in the future. © 2013 IEEE.},
