	affiliations = {University of Bahrain, Collage of Business and Administration, Sakheer, Bahrain},
	abstract = {Optimizing stock portfolios is one of the ultimate objectives of rational investors. This objective is achieved by identifying the stocks that are expected to perform well in future and then making decisions to allocate funds amongst them in order to create the optimal investment portfolio. In the past, this optimization problem was addressed with the help of different statistical tools that were static and limited. Due to technological advancements, the implementation of machine learning methods in this field has become increasingly popular, enabling researchers to solve complex and nonstationary problems. There have been many recent studies that have integrated hyperparameters tuning kernels with these algorithms and combined modified-assets-selection-models to improve portfolio optimization results. Therefore, this paper provides a focused literature review on implementing machine learning in optimizing stock portfolios for the years between 2019 and 2022, and Google scholar was used to search for the relevant studies from Q1 or Q2 journals. This review revealed that Long short-term memory model was the most applied machine learning algorithm in predicting stocks returns. Moreover, it was found that Markowitz mean-variance model (classic and modified) was the most adopted approach for portfolio optimization. Based on the literature review, future insights are highlighted, and opportunities for further research are identified. © 2023 IEEE.},
