	affiliations = {Department of Cognitive Sciences, University of California Irvine, Irvine, CA, United States; Department of Biomedical Informatics, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, New York, NY, United States; Program for Mathematical Genomics, Department of Systems Biology, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, New York, NY, United States; Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Columbia University Irving Medical Center, New York, NY, United States; CIFAR Azrieli Global Scholars Program, CIFAR, Toronto, Canada; Program in Brain, Mind, & Consciousness, CIFAR, Toronto, Canada},
	abstract = {Machine-learning models are key to modern biology, yet models trained on one dataset are often not generalizable to other datasets from different cohorts or laboratories due to both technical and biological differences. Domain adaptation, a type of transfer learning, alleviates this problem by aligning different datasets so that models can be applied across them. However, most state-of-the-artdomain adaptation methods were designed for large-scale data such as images, whereas biological datasets are smaller and have more features, and these are also complex and heterogeneous. This Review discusses domain adaptation methods in the context of such biological data to inform biologists and guide future domain adaptation research. We describe the benefits and challenges of domain adaptation in biological research and critically explore some of its objectives, strengths, and weaknesses. We argue for the incorporation of domain adaptation techniques to the computational biologist's toolkit, with further development of customized approaches. Copyright © 2024 The Authors, some rights reserved.},
	keywords = {Computational Biology; Databases, Factual; Humans; Machine Learning; Adversarial machine learning; Anthropogenic; Federated learning; Adaptation methods; Benefit and challenges; Biological data; Biological research; Domain adaptation; Large scale data; Machine learning models; Modern biology; Small scale; Transfer learning; bioinformatics; factual database; human; machine learning; procedures; Biotic},
