	journal = {Proceedings of International Conference on Contemporary Computing and Informatics, IC3I 2023},
	pages = {77 – 81},
	doi = {10.1109/IC3I59117.2023.10397889},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85185227617&doi=10.1109%2fIC3I59117.2023.10397889&partnerID=40&md5=488175823a01058850e6e8e4444d13b3},
	affiliations = {School of CSE, Lovely Professional University, Punjab, Phagwara, India},
	abstract = {Alzheimer's disease (AD) is a chronic and irreversible brain illness for which no effective treatment exists. However, available medicines can only slow the progress of the disease. Thus, stopping and regulating the progression of AD depends greatly on its early identification. Researchers have reported some amazing findings using computer-aided techniques, but there is no reliable diagnostic technique clinically. Deep models have gained popularity in the last few years, particularly when working with images. Since 2013 onwards, deep learning (DL)has started to receive significant attention in the detection of this disease, and hence most of the research on this disease is based on deep learning models. Deep learning models have apparently proven to be more effective at detecting AD than standard machine-learning algorithms and techniques. Even yet, diagnosis of this disease is still challenging, and categorizing as it requires highly discriminative characteristic representation to separate similar types of brain patterns. In this review paper, we focus on the findings of various research done in the last few years in the field of AD detection. Deep learning (DL) still has certain limitations, particularly in terms of training strategies and dataset accessibility, even though it has demonstrated excellent results in the diagnosis of AD.  © 2023 IEEE.},
