	abstract = {Artificial intelligence-based decision systems can remold medical science by performing complex tasks delegated to specialists to enhance diagnosis accuracy, increase throughput performance, reduce clinical workflow, and improve care options. Machine Learning (ML) is reshaping and transforming our healthcare systems by empowering machines to execute cognitive tasks to accomplish a specific objective based on data provision. Malignancy is the principal cause of fatalities in cancer patients due to invading of surrounding cancer cells’ tissue to different organs. Early detection and identification of malignant cancers are potentially crucial. This research aimed to conduct a systematic literature review study to analyze ML diagnostic accuracy studies based on detecting clinically relevant prostate cancer datasets from cytological features based on a digital scan. To handle the variability in real-world data, we explored using synthetic data generation upon the technique of Conditional Tabular generative adversarial networks (CTGAN). Many researchers have well-considered boosting methods for the risk prediction model of the disease. In this work, the Gradient Boosting (GB) algorithm is proposed by using both prostate cancer and synthetic datasets combinedly. The work’s objective is to analyze and differentiate between clinically significant and insignificant prostate cancer assessment through hyperparameter optimization for the GB algorithm by using the Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) technique. © 2023 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.},
