	affiliations = {Shanghai Normal University Tianhua College, China},
	abstract = {This study delves into the factors to influence users' intentions to continue using language learning applications, specifically examining the roles of perceived ease of use, perceived usefulness, and flow among Chinese college students. Drawing upon the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) and Flow Theory, this research aims to elucidate the mechanisms that drive users' intentions to persist in engaging with these apps. A sample of 134 Chinese college students participated in an online survey by filling out questionnaires concerning perceived ease of use, perceived usefulness, flow, and continuance intention. The findings confirm a strong positive relationship between perceived ease of use and continuance intention. Secondly, perceived ease of use emerges as a significant predictor, indirectly influencing continuance intention through perceived usefulness and flow. Thirdly, perceived usefulness and flow sequentially mediated the link between perceived ease of use and the continued intention to use language learning apps. These insights provide valuable guidance for app designers, emphasizing the importance of encouraging learners to utilize social functions within apps to enrich their engagement in interactive language learning experiences. © 2024 ACM.},
