	affiliations = {Srm Institute of Science and Technology, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Tamilnadu, Trichy, India},
	abstract = {The developments in deep learning have greatly influenced the processes of the identification of plant diseases, producing outstanding enhancements in terms of precision and functionality. The aims of this survey are to provide a comprehensive literature review of deep learning techniques in plant disease diagnosis and prognosis with special emphasis on recent development and future developments for further study. It provides critical analysis of the most popular models including CNNs, Transfer Learning, Few-Shot Learning and shares insights on how these technologies are applied in crops including rice, wheat and maize. These challenges include the lack of quality data, and the problem of transferring models from one environment to another, as well as the computational complexity that makes the application of these models in agriculture. Moreover, the investigation identifies the IoT and Edge Computing as a potential for the early disease diagnosis and surveillance. The paper also points out that the significance of Explainable AI or XAI is taking firm root; this is an innovation that seeks to explain the workings of models; thus, making it popular among stakeholders in agriculture. This literature from the last decade is analyzed to outline the research priorities and possibilities for future development in the design of new models, ways of sharing data, and ongoing field evaluation. This survey is to be used by the researchers and practitioners who are involved in developing sustainable and scalable approaches of managing plant diseases using deep learning technology. © 2024 IEEE.},
