	affiliations = {Adekunle Ajasin University, Faculty of Education, Arts Education Department, Ondo State, Akungba-Akoko, Nigeria; Bina Nusantara University, Binus Online Learning, Management Department, Jakarta, Indonesia; National Account, BPS-Statistics Indonesia, Jakarta, Indonesia; University of Malaya, Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences, Geography Department, Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia; Universiti Tunku Abdul Rahman, Faculty of Creative Industries, Game Studies Department, Selangor, Sungai Long, Malaysia; Burapha University International College, Burapha University, Hospitality, Tourism and Mice Management, Chonburi, Thailand},
	abstract = {This study investigates the benefits of virtual learning experiences in Nigeria post-during the pandemic, emphasizing antecedents such as geographical regions, the impact of the digital divide, recommendations, insights, and primary challenges on student perceptions. Utilizing an exploratory approach with a survey questionnaire, data was collected from a diverse sample of 200 students across educational levels. The results of these preliminary inductive investigations offer valuable perspectives and information that can guide decision-making processes for future academic planning and disaster readiness. The results indicate that different geographical regions and insights significantly influence perceived benefits, while the digital divide, primary challenges, and recommendations may not play a crucial role. Specifically, 51% of respondents find virtual learning content and interactions satisfactory. Additionally, 50% believe virtual learning fosters strong teacher-student bonds, enhancing overall learning efficiency. These findings offer valuable insights and recommendations for policymakers and educators aiming to improve the effectiveness, accessibility, and inclusivity of virtual learning in Nigeria's education system. The study contributes to the existing knowledge on virtual learning, informing decision-making for a more successful and inclusive virtual learning environment in Nigeria. © 2024 IEEE.},
