	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85216093558&doi=10.1109%2fISITDI62380.2024.10796672&partnerID=40&md5=2278b3aa6990258d1f78be45aa5aca6b},
	affiliations = {Universitas Gadjah Mada, Departement of Electrical Engineering and Information Technology, Yogyakarta, Indonesia; Universitas Gadjah Mada, Electrical and Information Engineering Departement, Yogyakarta, Indonesia},
	abstract = {Incomplete wind speed data presents a formidable obstacle in meteorological and renewable energy studies, potentially undermining the precision of related analyses and predictive models. This paper embarks on a systematic exploration of various imputation methods for mitigating the gaps in wind speed records, which frequently encounter discrepancies due to operational challenges in measurement apparatus. The overriding ambition of this discourse is to shed light on conceivable enhancements in extant machine learning models tailored for wind speed data imputation. In doing so, it aspires to furnish insights into prevailing trends and cultivate a more profound comprehension of efficacious strategies for estimating absent wind speed values, thereby contributing substantially to the advancement of this critical field of study. Some of the findings are that it is clear that the field is increasingly turning to machine learning (ML) and deep learning approaches to overcome the limitations of traditional statistical methods. A close examination of 21 articles reveals that Long Short Term Memory (LSTM) and Multivariate Imputation by Chained Equations (MICE) are the most widely developed at present and Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) as the main metric plays the performance of imputation techniques, reflecting the emphasis on achieving high accuracy in the imputation process. Future research should explore broader input data features, improve the feature selection process with refined correlation coefficients, and adopt rigorous hyperparameter tuning and sequential imputation methods leveraging ML algorithms. © 2024 IEEE.},
