	affiliations = {College of Engineering Roorkee, Roorkee, India; Coer University, Roorkee, India},
	abstract = {Wildlife and animal behaviour tracking and recording is a discipline that has received a lot of attention mainly because of the growing environmental changes and human activities that are influential in many ways to wildlife and their habitats. Former methods of data collection by direct observation, and handwriting, are now either supplemented or substituted by complex technological tools. This review paper also discusses different ones like deep learning models such as YOLOv8 for the constant monitoring of animal behaviour, hybrid models that combine VGG-19 and Bi-LSTM for real-time animal detection and alertness, as well as the multitask learning framework for efficient animal activity recognition using low-cost embedded systems. Such technologies provide potential strategies for tracking, identifying, improving the quality of life, and reducing the risk of potential incidences between humans and wildlife. Furthermore, the paper also enlightens the possibilities of applying CNN for sheep breed recognition, which plays a part in how machine learning helps enhance livestock use. The various accomplishments discussed in this paper reflect clear progress in the area of smart farming and wildlife protection and the technologies presented can be described as viable, practical, and feasible for implementation on a large scale. © 2024 IEEE.},
