	affiliations = {Rochester Institute of Technology, Department of Electrical and Microelectronics Engineering, Rochester, 14623, NY, United States; Pjm Interconnection, Audubon, 19403, PA, United States},
	abstract = {Maintaining situational awareness of the transmission network is of great importance to PJM. Therefore, PJM needs to be aware of new equipment being added to the grid following a scheduled transmission outage. Currently, PJM manually reviews outage tickets every morning to determine if new equipment is being energized, known as a cut-in. This process is error-prone and time-intensive. In this paper, natural language processing is used to perform binary text classification of transmission outage tickets to predict whether they include a cut-in or not. The key difficulty lies in the data imbalance caused by a significantly lesser number of tickets that have cut-ins. To address this difficulty, class weights which are based on the frequency of class occurrences are applied to the loss function while training the machine learning models. After cleaning and encoding textual data, both traditional and deep learning algorithms are used to classify tickets. Results show that XGBoost gives the best trade-off between false positives and false negatives.  © 2024 IEEE.},
