	abstract = {Depression is feelings of sadness, irritation or stress, it encompasses a series of psychological, physical and emotional symptoms that can disturb or even snatch the ability to function properly from an individual in their daily life. Approximately 280 million people which is around 3.8 percent of the world's population experiences the depression. But unfortunately, the stigma in our society about mental health issue often prevents individual from seeking help which leads a number of people experiencing depression being untreated and unidentified. Therefore, it is important to have a system which can predict depression. In this paper we implement five classification algorithms: Random Forest (RF), Decision Tree (DT), Support Vector Machine (SVM), K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN), Naïve Bayes (NB). Two datasets have been utilized for widening the perspective of our study. One of these datasets have been taken from Kaggle while we collect another dataset by conducting survey between the six reputed universities of India. In this paper we also use Synthetic Minority Over-Sampling Technique (SMOTE) to balance the datasets. Further we use grid search for hyperparameter tuning to optimize and increase the efficiency of model. In dataset 1 SVM and RF have outperformed other models while in dataset 2 DT have outperformed others algorithm.  © 2023 IEEE.},
