	pages = {165 – 178},
	doi = {10.1016/B978-0-443-22206-1.00010-3},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105002206885&doi=10.1016%2fB978-0-443-22206-1.00010-3&partnerID=40&md5=1642f29fa5c156e3f9925eabe495c42a},
	affiliations = {Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Mepco Schlenk Engineering College, Tamil Nadu, Sivakasi, India; Department of Mechanical Engineering, Amrita School of Engineering, Amrita Vishwa Vidyapeetham, Tamil Nadu, Coimbatore, India},
	abstract = {Biomedicine 4.0 combines big data, artificial intelligence, and other advanced technologies to improve medical diagnosis, treatment, and patient outcomes. Disease prognosis plays a critical role in Biomedicine 4.0, which involves predicting the future course and outcome of a disease based on several factors, such as the patient's medical history, symptoms, and test results. It helps healthcare professionals make informed decisions about treatment options, develop new treatments and interventions, and improve patient outcomes. However, there are challenges such as limited data, the complexity of diseases varying from person to person, and a lack of standardization. To overcome this limitation, machine learning and artificial intelligence algorithms are widely used for efficient analysis of medical data and forecasting disease outcomes. Machine learning-based disease prognosis can improve the accuracy and speed of disease diagnosis, treatment planning, and patient outcomes. In this chapter, we review commonly used machine learning algorithms to augment medical diagnosis and emphasize the significance of transfer learning. Subsequently, we emphasize the significance of transfer learning and highlight how it eliminates the need to build a learning model from scratch and produces better results than other machine learning (ML) methods. We also summarize how the sophistication of machine learning-based disease prognosis models facilitates healthcare professionals with more accurate prognostic models, improved treatment outcomes, and reduced healthcare costs. © 2025 Elsevier Inc. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
	author_keywords = {Big data; Biomedicine 4.0; Disease prognosis; Machine learning; Transfer learning},
