	doi = {10.1109/CATS58046.2023.10424179},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85186139172&doi=10.1109%2fCATS58046.2023.10424179&partnerID=40&md5=01612f1ee3fad657f6fea9bb5adf9365},
	affiliations = {University of Pennsylvania, PA, United States},
	abstract = {In recent years, the fields of Machine Learning (ML) and Artificial Intelligence (AI) have witnessed a transformative expansion, impacting various domains of science and technology. The advent of compact computational devices, notably smartphones, has led to a paradigm shift where computationally demanding tasks, such as computer vision and speech recognition, can be executed efficiently. A significant avenue of research is the deployment of neural networks on resource-constrained devices, particularly microcontrollers. These devices are often characterized by their limited performance, memory, and power capabilities. Neural Architecture Search (NAS) has emerged as a revolutionary approach to tailor network models for these constrained environments, obviating the need for manual and often error-prone adjustments by researchers [1]. While several comprehensive reviews on NAS exist [1], [2], there is a notable gap in literature regarding its application specifically to edge devices, such as microcontrollers (MCUs). This research aims to bridge this gap, offering a holistic overview of recent advancements in this niche. We contrast and compare various methodologies developed in recent years against benchmark applications tailored for mobile devices. Further, we elucidate potential avenues for future exploration and innovation in this domain.  © 2023 IEEE.},
